<Base title="Read the stones" description="Butkara, Barikot and Swat Museum: reading archaeological evidence across a living valley." section="heritage" image="butkara">
<section class="heritage-hero"><div class="page-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Deep time · Material evidence</span><h1>The ground<br/>remembers.</h1><p class="deck">A wall meets another wall. A sacred space is enlarged. A town is built again. History becomes visible in the joins.</p><a class="underlink" href="#layers">Read the layers ↓</a></div><Photo id="butkara" eager caption/></section>
<div class="reading-layout"><aside class="reading-aside"><span class="eyebrow">Read the stones</span><nav class="chapter-links"><a href="#butkara">Butkara I</a><a href="#layers">Barikot’s layers</a><a href="#collections">Objects & collections</a></nav></aside><article class="reading-copy"><section id="butkara"><h2>A sacred ground.</h2><p>At Butkara I, near Saidu Sharif, the arrangement of remains offers a way to read a Buddhist sacred complex. A central stupa stands among smaller votive stupas, columns and monastic structures.</p><p>The archaeological record identifies schist paving and a water tank as part of the site. These details bring attention back to the ground: to surfaces, routes and the organisation of a place used by people.</p><p>Gandharan heritage is encountered here through material evidence. The photograph records surviving remains; it does not reconstruct buildings or ceremonies that are no longer visible.</p><References ids={['butkara']}/></section><section id="layers"><h2>A town is never<br/>only one moment.</h2><p>At Barikot, excavation connects the hilltop acropolis with an ancient lower town. Open each layer of the story to see what different kinds of evidence can tell us.</p><div class="layer-list"><details open><summary>The hill and the plain</summary><div><p>The excavation team studies a settlement with both elevated and low-lying spaces. Their relationship matters: defensive position, habitation and the river landscape form parts of the same archaeological question.</p></div></details><details><summary>Building, adapting, reusing</summary><div><p>Successive phases left changes in walls, streets, housing and sacred architecture. Stratigraphy studies the order of deposits and structures. A later wall may reuse earlier material while changing how a space works.</p></div></details><details><summary>Evidence, still being read</summary><div><p>Excavation is an ongoing process of documentation and interpretation. An exposed wall is evidence; its date and function depend on its context, associated finds and comparison with the rest of the site.</p></div></details></div><div class="status-note">Bazira–Barikot entered Pakistan’s UNESCO Tentative List in May 2026. Tentative listing identifies a possible future nomination; it is not World Heritage inscription.</div><References ids={['excavation','barikot']}/><ArrowLink href="/places/barikot/" label="Visit the Barikot place note"/></section></article></div>
<section class="heritage-pair" id="collections"><Photo id="swat-museum" caption/><div><span class="eyebrow">Saidu Sharif · Swat Museum</span><h2>From ground<br/>to collection.</h2><p>Excavated objects have another life in a museum. Swat Museum conserves and presents Gandharan material alongside ethnographic collections of embroidery, carved wood and jewellery.</p><p>The collection connects archaeological study with the things people make and use. A museum object asks us to consider its context as well as its appearance.</p><References ids={['museum']}/><ArrowLink href="/places/mingora/" label="Mingora & nearby Saidu Sharif"/></div></section>
</Base>
